# Animation

- There is no animation library. Motion is CSS (transitions, keyframes, scroll-driven timelines, view transitions) or the WebGL canvas.
- One `requestAnimationFrame` loop exists, in `src/utils/loop.ts`. New per-frame work joins it instead of starting another loop.
- The canvas draws every glyph as a point sprite in a single draw call. Per-glyph motion belongs in the vertex shader, never in a JavaScript loop over glyphs.
- Every animation stops when `still()` is true. The Pause control sets it, and it starts true under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`. CSS animations are switched off under `.still` in `accessibility.css`.
- Scroll-driven and view-transition effects sit behind `@supports` or a feature check, and the page works without them.
- Work that only matters on screen is gated by an `IntersectionObserver`, as the render demo and the calendar do.
- If WebGL2 is unavailable or draws nothing, the page falls back to HTML headings under the `nogl` class. Keep that path working.
